C ionmvapsairoend, hwiigth tchoests,haonrtdcohimgihntgismoef cblonosdumanpatilyosni,s esxuhcahleads breath analysis has gradually become a promising diagnostic screening technology owing to its advantages of safety, noninvasive nature, convenient operation, and speediness, thus attracting much interest of researchers and clinicians.1−3 In human exhalation, hydrogen sulfide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}S\right)$ , a flammable, corrosive, and neurotoxic gas, has recently been considered an endogenous gas transmitter and a biomarker for the diagnosis of corresponding diseases.4 The concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in a healthy person’s breath is ${\sim}2\ \mathrm{ppb}$ ; the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration is more than $100~\mathrm{ppb}$ in the exhalation gas of halitosis patients.5 Although halitosis can simply be attributed to sulfur-related gases released by tongue plaque or microorganisms, it is an indication probe of more severe illnesses such as oral cavity problems (such as dental caries and periodontitis) and stomach diseases.6,7 Therefore, it is essential to develop sensing devices with high sensitivity and selectivity for accurate monitoring of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration in breath.
Chemiresistor gas sensors are widely used owing to their ease of preparation, high sensitivity, and simple backend electrical signal processing.8 Conventional semiconducting metal oxides, such as $\mathtt{C u O}_{\mathtt{i}}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{WO}_{3},$ ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4)}$ are widely used in gas sensors to detect harmful gases.9−12 However, poor selectivity and high operating temperature are still the cruxes of semiconducting metal oxide-based gas sensors, causing the practical indicators for the effective detection of exhaled air biomarkers unsatisfactory, especially in the sensitivity and detection limit of ppb-grade gases.13 In recent years, substantial progress has been made to improve the gas sensing property of materials at room temperature (RT). For example, ultraviolet (UV) light irradiation as a substitute for high-temperature heating has been recognized to stimulate basic adsorption/desorption reactions on semiconductor surfaces.14 Photon-induced photocurrent increases the conductivity and thus increases the number of free charge carriers in the materials.15 Heterojunctions such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cu}\bar{\mathrm{O}},$
Scheme 1. (a) Schematic Illustration for Fabricating TCN; (b) Experimental Design of the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ Gas Sensor and the Z-Type Heterostructure-Based Sensing Mechanism; (c) Detailed Structure of a Single Sensing Chip; and (d) Schematic Diagram for Expired Gas Monitoring
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2},$ and ${\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}/{\mathrm{WO}_{3}}$ have demonstrated promising ability in achieving satisfactory gas sensing performance at RT.16−18 UV irradiation has shown good ability to improving the sensing response, reversibility, and response speed.19 However, most of the reported heterojunctions applied in gas sensing are mainly focused on type I and type II, where the oxidation and reduction ability of holes and electrons are largely limited.20
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) with a three-dimensional (3D) structure have been widely studied as a promising candidate for constructing heterojunctions owing to their large specific surface area and potential active sites (e.g., terminal functional groups).21−24 Particularly, the intrinsic micro-/ mesopores in the architectures enable MOFs to achieve excellent adsorption and transport capabilities for small molecules.25 This is favorable for preconcentrating the target analytes and thus leading to improved responses.26 In addition, many tunable functional ligands on MOFs enable them to achieve good selectivity in gas adsorption.27 This feature makes MOFs attractive for overcoming the specificity challenge in constructing gas sensors.28 Recently, Zhang’s group has applied Al-MIL-53- $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , an aluminum-based MOF, for selectively monitoring $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ .29 Jayaramulu et al. used amine-functionalized UiO-66 to detect $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ that exhibited good selectivity in volatile organic compounds.30 Even so, the reported MOF-based chemiresistor sensors suffer from low sensitivity to analytes or require high operating temperatures.
It is well known that $z$ -scheme heterojunctions not only ensure effective spatial separation of electron holes but also maintain the higher reduction of electrons and higher oxidation of photogenerated holes for some substantial applications.31−33 Therefore, accurate construction of $Z\cdot$ - scheme heterojunctions is alternative and meaningful to achieve advanced sensitivity in gas sensing. In this study, by combining the heterojunction on well-aligned $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanotube arrays (NTs) with amide $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ ) group-functionalized CoMOFs, we developed a sensitive and selective chemiresistor gas sensor for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ sensing. In this system, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs served as one of the semiconductive partners to fabricate $Z$ -scheme heterojunctions as well as a support matrix for growing MOFs. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-O}$ species serves as the other semiconductor partner for Z-scheme heterojunctions. Furthermore, the MOF layer also acts as an adsorbent for the preconcentration and selective response of target gas molecules. Considering a proofof-concept application, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ was selected as the analyte of interest, and Co-MOFs (Co-BDC and $\mathrm{Co-BDC-NH}_{2},$ where $\mathrm{BDC}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}=2$ -aminoterephthalic acid) were grown on the wall of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs using $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as the precursor to react with the corresponding organic ligands (Scheme 1). For the first time, the $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH_{2}/T i O_{2}}$ heterostructure combining with UV excitation reached ultrahigh sensitivity to ppb-level $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ gas at RT, which is of great essence for environmental monitoring and clinical applications.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Preparation of $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ NTs. Ti foils were ultrasonically cleaned in acetone, ethanol, and deionized (DI) water for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ each, and the clean Ti foils were then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs were prepared by the electrochemical anodization of cleaned Ti foils in ethylene glycol/lactic acid electrolyte containing 0.1 M $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ at $120{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for 4 min.34 The obtained $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs were washed with ethanol and dried in air. The samples were further annealed at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to transfer the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs to anatase crystalline.
Synthesis of $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}/\mathsf{C o}$ -MOF NTs. Before the coating of Coprecursor, the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs was hydroxylated in $3\Bar{0}\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ was synthesized on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs via a two-step procedure: (i) hydrolysis for forming a $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ film on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs and (ii) solvothermal transfer of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH_{2}}$ . Briefly, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs were immersed in $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ solution $(3,5,10,$ and $30~\mathrm{mM}$ ) and the entire system was kept in a water bath at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ for the hydrolysis of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{C}\bar{\mathrm{O}}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ to form the Co precursor on tube walls. The samples were thoroughly rinsed with DI water and then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The as-formed sample was immersed in a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ dimethylformamide (DMF)/methanol $(9{:}1,\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ mixture containing $10\ \mathrm{mg\BDC\cdotNH_{2}}$ and $2\mathrm{mM}$ HCl. The sealed vessel was held at 150 $^{\circ}{\bf C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ for growing Co-MOFs on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs. The resulting CoMOF-coated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs sample was thoroughly washed with DMF and ethanol to remove the unreacted ligands and then dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove the remaining solvent. For convenience, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{CoBDC}.$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ NTs were denoted $\mathrm{TCN}{-}x$ (where $x\ =\ 3,\ 5,\ 10,$ and 30 corresponding to 3, 5, 10, and $30~\mathrm{mM}$ of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ used for the preparation of the Co precursor). For comparison, $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C o B D C}$ NTs were prepared in the same manner, except replacing ${\mathrm{BDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ with BDC. The as-obtained samples were aged for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ at RT before further application.
Gas Sensing Measurements. Prior to the resistance measurement, two silver dots with a diameter of $1\ \mathrm{mm}$ and a distance of 0.5 cm were placed on the surface of sensors. The resistance of the sample was recorded using a CGS-MT analysis system (Beijing Elite Tech Co., Ltd., China), and the output signal was recorded using a computer. The schematic diagram of the gas sensor measurement is shown in Figure S1. The sensor response is defined as $R\:=\:R_{\mathrm{a}}/R_{\mathrm{g}},$ where $R_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{g}}$ represent the sample resistance in air and test gas, respectively. The response time $(\tau_{\mathrm{res}}^{-})$ is the time required to reach $90\%$ of the equilibrium resistance in air after exposure to the target gaseous molecules. The recovery time $(\tau_{\mathrm{rec}})$ is the time required to return to $90\%$ of resistance in the air after removing the target gaseous molecules. UV light irradiation was conducted using a light-emitting diode (LED) light (3 W). The irradiation intensity was controlled by adjusting the distance between the sample and the LED light. All the experiments were carried out at RT.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ Gas Sensor Fabrication. Scheme 1a shows the outline of fabricating a flexible $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ gas sensor based on a $_\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C o-M O F}$ NTs heterojunction. The corresponding detection mechanism is shown in Scheme 1b. With an MOF layer covering, the resulted NTs have a larger specific surface area and high electron mobility, thus providing more active sites for gas molecule adsorption. $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules are expected to access the $\mathbf{Co-MOF/TiO}_{2}$ interfaces. The unique functional group $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ on $_\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C o-M O F}$ is favorable for selective detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules.35 Importantly, the formation of Zscheme heterojunctions between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and Co-MOF facilitates the effective separation of electrons and holes, thus triggering the sensing reaction under UV irradiation. Such a sensing device can be fabricated on a flexible Ti base (Scheme 1c). Owing to its excellent flexibility and wearability, this device is expected to be well coated onto the human wrist and arm. The schematic diagram of the chemiresistor gas sensor is shown in Scheme 1d. It can directly detect $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules in breath. When the target gas reaches the sensing area, the response is easily read out by a resistance meter.
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs and COBDC- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -modified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs (denoted as TCN)were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). As shown in Figure 1a, the as-obtained $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs exhibited a uniformed tubular structure with an inner diameter of ${\sim}150~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S2a). The NTs were vertically grown on the Ti substrate, and the length was ${\sim}4~\mu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure 1b). The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ hydroxylation process induced a large amount of $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs, as shown by the negative shift of zeta potential (Figure S3). These negative charges enabled abundant ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions to be easily grafted onto the tube wall via electrostatic adsorption. After hydrolysis, numerous tiny NPs were densely dispersed on the tube wall and entrance (Figure $S2b{\mathrm{-d}}{\dot{\mathrm{}}}$ ). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure S4) indicated that this $C_{0}$ precursor can be indexed to ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ (JCPDS no. 43-1003). Given suitable temperature and acidity, these Co-precursor NPs dissociated and participated in the reaction with the organic ligand ${\tt B D C}{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ to form CoBDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Figure 1c shows the SEM images of TCN. The sample shows a thicker tube wall. In transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Figure 1d), the lamellar $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals could be clearly distinguished. Figure 1e shows the highresolution TEM (HRTEM) image of TCN. The lattice fringes of $0.35\ \mathrm{nm}$ can be ascribed to the crystal planes (101) of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The lattice fringes of Co-MOF are not apparent (the unstable feature of Co-MOF under a strong electron beam illumination makes the imaging more difficult). In Figure 1f, the elemental mapping images of TCN demonstrate the coexistence of Ti, O, N, and Co elements. A homogeneous distribution of Co and $\mathbf{N}$ confirms the uniform growth of $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ on the entire NT membrane. Figure $_{1\mathrm{g}}$ shows the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs and TCN. All the diffraction peaks acquired on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs could be indexed to the anatase crystalline (JCPDS no. 21-1272) and Ti substrate. The characteristic diffraction peaks that appeared at ${\sim}6.9$ and $9.8^{\circ}$ can be ascribed to $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2},$ consistent with the previous report.36,37 The formation of $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ was also confirmed by Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (Figures 1h and S5a). The band at $3300{-}3500~\mathrm{\bar{cm}^{-1}}$ can be attributed to the stretching mode of amino groups; the two bands at 1670 and $1356~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be assigned to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-O}}$ , respectively.38,39 These results indicate the formation of $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals. Similarly, the successful synthesis of CoBDC nanocrystals was confirmed by the FTIR spectra (Figure S5b, the product is denoted as TN). Figure 1i shows the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption measurements of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs and TCN. After the coating of CoBDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ the surface area of NTs increased from 30.26 to $78.19\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The largely increased surface area indicates that the asproposed TCN sample has excellent potential in promoting target gas adsorption, thus achieving a sensitive response.
Gas Sensing Performance. To evaluate the effect of $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ loading amount on the sensing performance toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ the response of pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs and TCN- $x$ against $10\:\mathrm{ppm}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ was measured at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $365~\mathrm{{nm}~\mathrm{{UV}}}$ irradiation (3 W LED). As shown in Figure 2a, the response of TCN-10 reached a higher value (32), much larger than those of pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs (3.0), TCN-3 (12.8), TCN-5 (20.0), and TCN-30 (6.5). The following gas sensing was performed using TCN-10 as the sensing electrode. The absorption spectra of bare $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NTs, TC, TCN, and CoBDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were recorded (Figure S6a). $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs have a clear absorption band edge at $380\ \mathrm{nm}$ , consistent with the wide band gap of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{\sim}3.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ . The resulting TCN showed substantial absorption in both UV and visible-light regions $\mathrm{'}500\mathrm{-}700\ \mathrm{nm}\mathrm{,}$ . A high absorption in the visible-light region can be ascribed to the $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ coating. On the basis of the Tauc plot, the corresponding band gaps $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ were determined as 3.2 and $2.{\overset{\sim}{5}}{\mathrm{~eV}},$ respectively (Figure S6b). Encouraged by the good light absorption in the UV and visible-light regions, the sensing response of TCN-10 toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ was investigated under a series of LED lamps (3 W) at different wavelengths including 365, 465, 525, and $625~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Figure S7). The sample provided the highest response when $365~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation light was used. A similar phenomenon was also recorded on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs. These results indicate that the photocatalytic activity of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate provides most of the energy required for activating the gas-sensing reactions.40,41 The response of TCN-10 toward $10\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ when irradiated with $365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light with different intensities, was also evaluated (Figure S8). The sensing response was found to increase with increasing light intensity. Therefore, $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ LED with an irradiation intensity of 46 $\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ was used as the light source in the following gas sensing tests.
Figure $^{2\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c}}$ shows the sensing performances of bare $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NTs, TC-10, and TCN-10 toward different concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in dark and under UV-light irradiation, respectively. Apparently, all the samples exhibited an improved response when exposed to UV light. For example, the response of TCN under LED irradiation for $10\ \mathrm{ppm\H}_{2}S$ is 2.31 times higher than that in dark. Notably, compared to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs, the as-obtained $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NTs show a clear increased response to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in dark as well as under irradiation. The improved $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ response might be originated from the generated heterojunction between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the Co precursor. Compared with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ response from different samples, the response of TCN is larger than that of the other samples not only in dark but also under UV-light irradiation. The improved response can be ascribed to the preconcentration effect of MOF structures and the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups present on TCN. The dynamic responses and resistance of the TCN-10 sensor in the absence and presence of UV irradiation were further studied. Compared with the dynamic response values of TCN in dark (Figure S9), the sample exhibited a larger response when applying UV irradiation (Figure 2d).
The selectivity, relative humidity (RH), and long-term stability are important criteria for determining the practical applications of gas sensors. Figure 2e shows the sensing responses of the TCN sample toward different gases. Compared with the sensing response to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ $(10\ \mathrm{ppm})$ , the TCN sample shows very poor response even at a high concentration of $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{7}{\mathrm{H}}_{8},$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH},$ HCHO, $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , and NO (100 or $1000\ \mathrm{ppm},$ ). To further evaluate the anti-interference ability of the as-proposed gas sensing device in practical applications, TCN-10 was further used to measure the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ content in the mixed gases (mixed gas I: 10 ppm $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{;}$ , $100\ \mathrm{ppm\NO}$ , and $100\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{NH}_{3};$ mixed gas II: $10\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ $100\ \mathrm{ppm\C_{7}H_{8}},$ and 100 ppm $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH} $ ; mixed gas III: $10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , 100 ppm ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{5}{\mathrm{OH}}_{;}$ and 100 ppm HCHO). The results are shown in Figure 2f. The response in these mixtures did not display obvious variations when compared to the response in 10 ppm $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , verifying the excellent anti-interference ability of the sensor. The effect of RH on sensing was also explored (Figure S10), exhibiting a decreased response with increasing environmental RH. This phenomenon can be attributed to the competitive adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules. In this case, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules reacted with adsorbed $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ on the surface and formed the surface hydroxyls $\left(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\right)$ , which might act as barriers to block $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption. Despite this, the gas-sensing response was still as high as $60\%$ at a high RH of $90\%$ . The long-term stability of TCN-10 sensor was also measured (Figure S11). Gas response showed a slight attenuation trend during the two month testing period; however, the response attenuation ratio (defined as $\left({\cal R}_{0}-{\cal R}_{\mathrm{f}}\right)/{\cal R}_{0},$ where $R_{0}$ and $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ are the initial and final gas-sensing responses, respectively) was only $9.2\%$ . Following this finding, excellent selectivity, superior humidity tolerance, and long-term stability demonstrated that the as-prepared TCN is promising for practical applications.
Effect of Light Illumination on Sensing Response. To evaluate the effect of UV-light irradiation on improving the TCN sensing response in $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ , the response/recovery time and response linear range were studied in dark and under irradiation. Figure 3a shows the response curves of TCN-10 toward $10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in dark and under UV-light irradiation. In the dark, the response and recovery times were determined as 448 and $^{1043\ s,}$ respectively. Under UV irradiation, the response and recovery times decreased to 209 and ${296\ s,}$ respectively. The response and recovery times decreased to ${\sim}50$ and ${\sim}28\%$ if this system was exposed to UV light, respectively. Figure 3b shows the gas response of TCN-10 toward $0.025{-}50~\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in the absence and presence of UV-light irradiation. In both the conditions, the gas sensing responses show an increasing tendency with the increase in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration. Compared with the gas response in dark, the sensing response was substantially improved under UV irradiation. The samples exhibited a good response linear range from $25~\mathrm{ppb}$ to $1.0\ \mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 3c). The enhanced response became more remarkable under illumination. For instance, when the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration was $0.25~\mathrm{ppm}$ , the response in dark and under irradiation was 1.4 and 4.3, respectively. The limit of detection (LOD) could be calculated using eq 1
$$
\mathrm{LOD}=3\sigma/s
$$
where $\sigma$ is the standard deviation and $s$ is the slope of the calibration curve.42 On the basis of the fitting curve shown in Figure 3c, the LOD values were determined as 1.3 and 11.6 ppb for the measurements performed under UV irradiation and in dark, respectively. These results show that UV radiation can effectively shorten the response/recovery times and increase the sensing sensitivity, favorable features for the detection of a low concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in exhaled breath. The successive dynamic response/recovery sensing behavior at $10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ and 250 ppb $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{-}}$ , respectively, was studied by five sensing cycles under UV light and in dark (Figures 3d, S12, and S13). For both cases, no significant changes were noticed in the sensing responses toward 10 ppm $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{,}}$ , demonstrating excellent repeatability of gas sensing devices. Furthermore, the responses from 10 different TCN samples showed satisfactory uniformity and reproducibility (Figure S14). These results indicate a reliable application of the sensor in target $\mathrm{\mathrm{H}}_{2}S$ gas determination.
Generally, real applications are the final goal for sensor construction. Encouraged by the ultralow response concentration toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and excellent anti-interference ability of the as-proposed TCN, we measured the oral $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ levels of healthy volunteers before and after brushing. As shown in Figure 3e, the average response of the as-proposed sensing device to morning exhalation (the tester did not brush the teeth) was recorded as 2.7 under UV-light irradiation, corresponding to 75 ppb $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . This value is consistent with the previously reported physiological halitosis (morning halitosis).35 Notably, the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in the oral cavity decreased substantially after brushing. There was only a slight fluctuation of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ level in the oral cavity $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ after brushing. Furthermore, the oral $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ levels in the exhaled breath of people with severe periodontitis were also studied under UV light (Figure S15). The response to the breath of periodontitis patients was apparently higher than that of healthy people. According to the calibration curves under UV-light irradiation, the corresponding $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentrations (before brushing and in periodontitis patients) were calculated as 200 and $30\mathrm{\pp}$ . Notably, these results were in well agreement with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentrations that were determined by the classical titration method (Figure 3f), suggesting the reliability and practicability of the as-proposed chemiresistor gas sensor in exhalation detection. The above results indicate that the TCN-based sensing device can not only identify physiologic halitosis reliably and accurately but also monitor oral diseases and periodontal problems in time.
Sensing Mechanism. To understand the effect of photoassisted activation on the gas-sensing reaction, the $I{-}V$ characteristics of TCN in air under UV light and dark conditions were compared (Figure S16). The sensor showed a negligible low current in the dark (e.g., $0.16\mathrm{\nA}$ at $0.5{\mathrm{~V}}_{,}^{\prime}$ ). Notably, the current was substantially increased under UV illumination (e.g., $2.8~\mathrm{nA}$ at $0.5\mathrm{\:V}$ ), 17.5-fold higher than the current in dark. The largely enhanced electron concentration can be attributed to the photogenerated carriers under UV irradiation. The first-order kinetic models were used to estimate the rate constant $(k)$ from the response plot of TCN-10 to $10\ \mathrm{ppm\H}_{2}S$ in the presence and absence of light (Figure S17). The deduced rate constant between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and TCN-10 was $1.4\times10^{-3}\:\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ in the dark, in comparison to $1.2\times$ $10^{-2}\ s^{-1}$ under UV irradiation. The rate constant under UV irradiation was nearly 8 times that of in the dark, indicating that the gas sensing reaction under UV irradiation was more feasible than that in the dark.
For gas molecules, their adsorption properties on nonporous and porous substrates are the key to obtain a high sensitivity. As shown in Figure $\mathsf{4a}_{,\mathsf{4a}_{\ell}}$ , the collisions between gas molecules and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NTs can be considered as elastic, by which the contact between gas molecules and ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanoparticles is completed in an extremely short time. After this impacted action, most of the gas molecules retain most of the kinetic energy and float away with their original velocity. On the TCN surface (Figure 4b), the collision frequency can be enlarged on the large-increased surface area because of MOF modification, and the intact time is expected to be prolonged owing to the microporous structure of MOFs.43 Therefore, the increase in the collision frequency and time enables more targeted gaseous molecules to react and absorb in $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ . Therefore, compared with $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, porous $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH_{2}}$ can adsorb more $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ gas molecules, showing better gas sensitivity. After converting the Co precursor to CoBDC and CoBDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ the sensing ability is recorded to be larger on the sample with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ functionalization. In the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ atmosphere, the dissociated adsorption reaction occurs between $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ gas molecules.44 The reaction can be expressed as eq 2. As an electron-contributing reaction, the reaction could transfer additional electrons to the conduction band (CB) to cause an increase in the electron concentration, leading to further reduction in resistance, thus inducing a superior sensing performance on TCN.
$$
-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{+H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\Leftarrow-\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{HS}
$$
The possible interface interaction and charge transfer mechanism of TCN are shown in Figure 4c. When $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs and $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ came in contact with each other, the type II heterojunction could be established at the interface of $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C o B D C\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ NTs, in which the electrons migrated from $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs by band bending until the Fermi level reached the equilibrium (Figure S18). Therefore, an “electron depletion layer” was generated on the surface of $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ and “an electron accumulation layer” on the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs. A built-in electric field (EF) was thus built because of the redistribution of electrons between $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NTs. Upon UV-light irradiation, the electrons accept photon energy, and then they are excited to the CB. The built-in EF- and potential-barrier-induced photogenerated electrons in ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{CB}}$ recombined with the photogenerated holes in CN-VB to form the Z-scheme heterojunction. As a result, the separation of photogenerated electron−hole pairs was effectively facilitated, thus retaining abundant highly energetic electrons for the subsequent gassensitive reactions.32
When the samples were kept in air, oxygen molecules $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ were adsorbed on the heterojunction. These adsorbed $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules captured electrons $\left(\mathrm{e}^{-}\right)$ from the CB of semiconductors. Therefore, an electron depletion layer was formed on the surface of the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C o B D C\cdot N H_{2}}$ heterojunction. This process generally induced an increased resistance. The reactions can be described as follows
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{(gas)}\to\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{(ads)}}}\\ {{\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{(ads)}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\to\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{^{-}}\mathrm{(ads)}}}\end{array}
$$
However, most metal oxides and MOFs are insulators rather than semiconductors at RT, leading to a higher reaction barrier and fewer available electrons in the heterojunction. Therefore, the sensing reaction is hard to occur on the heterojunction because of the lack of sufficient reactive oxygen species.45 As shown in Figure $\mathsf{4d}_{\cdot}$ , when the sensing system was exposed to UV light, the required energy for the related sensing reaction can be provided by the electron−hole pairs generated in both $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ via photocatalysis. These photogenerated carries participate in the gas sensing reactions (as shown in eqs 5 and 6).46
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{h\nu\rightarrow\mathrm{e}^{-}(h\nu)+\mathrm{h}^{+}(h\nu)}}\\ {{{\mathrm{O}_{2}}^{-}+\mathrm{h}^{+}(h\nu)\rightarrow{\mathrm{O}_{2}}^{\bullet}(\mathrm{gas})}}\end{array}
$$
The desorption of $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet}(\mathrm{gas})$ has been recognized as a facile route that releases electrons into the CB, thus resulting in a decrease in resistance. Notably, a high electron population might promote the adsorption of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ because oxygen showed a high electron affinity and a low chemical potential. The “refreshed” electrons $\left(\mathrm{e}^{-}\right)$ could trap more adsorbed oxygen, generating “refreshed” oxygen ions $\left[\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)\right]$ (eq 7). These “refreshed” oxygen ions $\left[\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)\right]$ as active sites on the interface heterojunction were deemed to be more energetic with higher activity.16
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{e}^{-}(h\nu)\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{2\mathrm{H}_{2}S+3\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+2\mathrm{SO}_{2}+3\mathrm{e}^{-}}}\end{array}
$$
In $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ gas, as shown in Figure 4e, ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)$ reacts with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on the heterojunction surface (eq 8). This reaction released the captured electrons into the CB to refill. The increasing electron concentration indicates a reduction in the thickness of the depletion layer, resulting in a decrease in the resistance. As a result, a satisfactory $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ sensing performance was achieved at RT.
Mechanical Stability. To explore the application potential of the gas sensor as a flexible device, the mechanical stability was studied. In Figure 5a, the TCN-based gas sensing device with the size of $1\mathrm{{cm}\times1}$ cm was grown directly on an ultrathin titanium sheet with good flexibility. The distance of the $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ electrode to the sensor was $0.5\ \mathrm{cm}$ . The photographs clearly show that the resultant sensing device can be bent with bending radii of 10, 15, and $20~\mathrm{mm}$ . The sensing performances at different bending radii were measured (Figure 5b). Encouragingly, the flexible sensor in different bending and flat states maintained the same dynamic sensing performance for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ (Figure $\mathfrak{s c}$ ). The bending resistance retention $\left(\mathrm{RR}_{\mathrm{b}}\right)$ can be expressed as eq 9
$$
\mathrm{RR_{b}}={R_{\mathrm{b}}}/{R_{\mathrm{f}}}\times100\%
$$
where $R_{\mathrm{b}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ are the output resistances of the TCN sensor under bent and flat conditions, respectively. The resistance retention of the sensor exhibited ignorable changes with the changes in different bending angles (Figure 5d). Furthermore, after the sample was bent for 50, 100, 200, 300, and 400 cycles, the resistance retention rate did not show a substantial decrease (Figure 5e). The results indicate that the as-proposed TCN has good flexibility and mechanical stability. Compared with other recently published $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ sensors (Table S1), TCN exhibits a higher sensitivity and a lower LOD, satisfying the requirements of trace detection.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, a $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ sensor was developed based on a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CoBDC-NH}_{2}$ heterojunction. With UV-assisted excitation, the as-formed $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ sensor showed ultrasensitivity at RT. The synergistic effect of the nanotubular architecture and the amino-functionalized Co-MOF layer resulted in a convenient electronic transmission and many reactivated sites for promoting gas adsorption, diffusion, and reaction. Particularly, upon exposure to UV irradiation, the conversion of type II heterojunction to the Z-scheme heterojunction largely facilitated the separation of electron−hole pairs, thus providing plenty of electrons for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ sensing reactions. The resulting TCN-10 showed excellent gas sensing performance at RT, including a high sensitivity response $(\bar{R_{\mathrm{air}}}/\bar{R}_{\mathrm{gas}}=32{-}10\ \mathrm{ppm})$ , a low detection limit $\left(1.31\mathrm{\ppb}\right)$ , superior selectivity, repeatability, and long-term stability. We expect that the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C o B D C\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ sensor can not only monitor $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in the environment but also be useful for detecting $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in the breath, opening up a new application for noninvasive medical diagnosis.
